  "authors": "Shaashua Lee, Anabel Eckerling, Boaz Israeli, Gali Yanovich, Ella Rosenne, Suzana Fichman-Horn, Ido Ben Zvi, Liat Sorski, Ronit Satchi-Fainaro, Tamar Geiger, Erica K Sloan, Shamgar Ben-Eliyahu",
  "field_subfield": "Oncology; Metastasis Biology",
  "type_of_paper": "Original Research Article",
  "executive_summary": "This study investigates the mechanisms behind the spontaneous regression of metastases following primary tumor (PT) excision, a phenomenon previously documented in case studies but lacking a mechanistic model. Using a highly sensitive luciferase-expressing MDA-MB-231 HM breast cancer model in BALB/c nu/nu mice, the authors demonstrate that removing the primary tumor leads to a marked regression of early-stage micro-metastases, while larger metastases remain unaffected. This regression is not due to surgical trauma, as sham surgery did not induce similar effects. Instead, the study identifies the primary tumor secretome as a critical support system for early metastatic growth. In vitro, tumor-conditioned medium enhanced cancer cell viability, adhesion, and endothelial tube formation. In vivo, replenishing the secretome via osmotic minipumps after PT excision significantly diminished metastatic regression. Proteomic and cytokine array analyses identified four key secreted factors: IL-8, PDGFaa, Serpin E1 (PAI-1), and MIF. In vivo neutralization of these four factors arrested micro-metastasis development even in the presence of the primary tumor. Furthermore, analysis of the TCGA lung adenocarcinoma dataset revealed that high protein levels of these four factors correlate with poor patient survival (hazard ratio 2.05). These findings suggest that the primary tumor actively supports early metastatic niches via secreted factors, and that targeting these factors during the perioperative period could be a novel strategy to control minimal residual disease.",
  "research_context": [
    { "Surgical removal of the primary tumor is a cornerstone of cancer treatment.": "cornerstone of cancer treatment" },
    { "However, various case studies report the opposite effect; spontaneous postoperative regression of evident cancer metastasis.": "spontaneous postoperative regression" },
    { "Unfortunately, postulated mechanisms have not been empirically tested, as no animal model of spontaneous regression exists.": "no animal model" },
    { "The need to study potential interactions between the primary tumor (PT) and metastases is stressed by the current realization that the presence of a primary tumor is a systemic disease.": "systemic disease" }
  ],
  "research_question_and_hypothesis": [
    { "Herein, for the first time, we present an animal model of spontaneous regression of metastases following PT removal.": "animal model of spontaneous regression" },
    { "We found that development of micro-metastases is supported by the numerous factors secreted from the PT.": "supported by the numerous factors" },
    { "We hypothesized that the primary tumor secretome supports the survival and growth of distant micro-metastases.": "supports the survival and growth" },
    { "We hypothesized that if these four factors are among those that drive metastatic growth, their in vivo blockade would partly mimic the effect of primary tumor removal.": "partly mimic the effect" }
  ],
  "methodological_details": [
    { "A highly metastatic variant of the triple-negative breast adenocarcinoma cell line, MDA-MB-231 HM, was transduced with a codon-optimized firefly luciferase-mCherry vector.": "MDA-MB-231 HM" },
    { "Eight-week old female BALB/c nu/nu mice were injected with 2×10^5 cells in 20 μl PBS into the fourth mammary fat pad.": "2×10^5 cells" },
    { "Once metastatic foci reached a total count of 10^6 photons/s (~ 3-4 weeks post injection), the primary tumor (average size of 80mm^3) was excised.": "10^6 photons/s" },
